  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 AM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nThe mid-level disturbance that has been affecting us has mainly \npushed off to the northwest, though some lingering energy remains \nalong the Rio Grande, and could see a showers/storms continue there \nthrough tonight. Otherwise expect mainly quiet conditions overnight. \nAt the surface, winds have shifted to the northeast, but the surface \nboundary remains well to the northwest.  The boundary will sink into \nthe area tonight, but modify substantially and we will likely not \nsee anything noticeable with it's passage. The boundary is then \nexpected to stall near the coast, but most likely just to the east \nof the coastline. This will keep unsettled weather in the area for \nthe next several days. Deep east and southeast flow will continue \nto usher moisture into South Texas. Tomorrow will increase rain \nchances in the afternoon as a wave of moisture supported by mid-\nlevel low pressure over the southern Gulf pushes in. Medium to high \nrain chances continue on Sunday, especially in the afternoon. By \nMonday the mid-level ridging along with the boundary will begin \ndrifting eastward in response to an approaching shortwave. This will \nkeep rain chances mainly confined to the coast or offshore. Another \nboundary drops into the area mid-week with this shifting pattern and \nis currently expected to bring an end to the wet pattern. That said, \nthere is considerable uncertainty in how the end of the week looks \nwith some guidance suggesting coastal troughing and onshore flow \nreturning to South Texas and increasing rain chances once again. \n\nThe above mentioned unsettled pattern will keep temperatures under \ncontrol through the weekend and into the early part of next week \nwith highs mainly in the 80s area wide. Unfortunately, with the \nelevated moisture, low temps will continue to be at summer-like \nlevels in the mid 70s. As the column dries a bit the middle of next \nweek low temps could cool a few degrees...but we'll also warm temps \na few degrees...back to a bit above normal. \n\nDue to the persistent northeasterly flow enhancing the Ekman \ntransport mechanism and increasing 7-8 second swell periods, minor \ncoastal flooding is worth monitoring this weekend, especially during \nhigh tide at night into the early morning hours.",
